Overview [#overview]
********************
Jig is a better Fantom test runner.

Jig wraps 'fant' with output you can scan: results grouped by pod and test
class, a live status bar pinned to the bottom of the terminal, and failures
printed with only the output and stack frames that matter.

  acme
    AddrTest       [ 7/ 7] pass (3ms)
    ScheduleTest   [ 6/ 6] pass (37ms)
    UserTest       [ 5/ 5] pass (2ms)
    SyncTest       [ 3/ 3] pass (6.0sec)
    CsvReaderTest  [10/10] pass (10ms)
    InvoiceTest    [12/12] pass (1278ms)
    ───────────────────────────
    ✓ 43 passed (7.4sec)

  acmeWeb
    SessionTest    [ 8/ 8] pass (405ms)
    RenderTest     [16/16] pass (3475ms)
    AssetTest      [ 4/ 4] pass (55ms)
    RouteTest      [22/22] pass (9ms)
    ───────────────────────────
    ✓ 50 passed (3973ms)

  ✓ All 93 tests passed [601 verifies] in 11.5sec

Install [#install]
******************
Install from [Eggbox]`https://eggbox.fantomfactory.org` with 'fanr':

  fanr install jig

If your 'etc/fanr/config.props' doesn't default to Eggbox, pass the repo:

  fanr install -r https://eggbox.fantomfactory.org/fanr/ jig

Run this from outside any workenv (a directory with no 'fan.props' above
it). 'fanr' installs into the current env's 'lib/fan/', so outside a workenv
jig lands in your Fantom home and 'fan jig' works from any workenv.

To build from source instead, copy the pod into your Fantom home
('fan -version' prints the 'fan.home' path):

  fan src/jig/build.fan
  cp lib/fan/jig.pod <fan.home>/lib/fan/

Run as jig [#bin]
=================
On macOS or Linux, save this script as 'jig' in the 'bin/' directory of the
Fantom install you want it to use, next to 'fan' and 'fant' ('fan -version'
prints the 'fan.home' path), and make it executable with 'chmod +x':

  #!/bin/sh
  . "${0%/*}/fanlaunch"
  fanlaunch Fan jig "$@"

Now 'jig test acme' works the same as 'fan jig test acme'. Like 'fant' and 'fanr', it
runs with the Fantom install it lives in, not whichever 'fan' is first on
your 'PATH'. Don't symlink it elsewhere, since it finds 'fanlaunch' next to
itself.

Usage [#usage]
**************
  jig test <targets>* [options]
  jig build [<pod> | <file.fan>]* [options]

Options may come before or after targets. Exit code is '0' on success,
'1' otherwise.

test [#test]
============
Test targets use the same syntax as 'fant':

  jig test acme                     # all tests in pod
  jig test acme::FooTest            # all tests in a type
  jig test acme::FooTest.testBar    # a single test method
  jig test                          # all pods in workDir lib/fan/

Options:

  - '-q', '--quiet': only print failures, status bar, and summary
  - '-es': test new ECMA JavaScript environment (passthrough)
  - '-js': test legacy JavaScript environment (passthrough)

jig test vs fant -all [#work]
-----------------------------
'fant -all' runs every pod the env can resolve, including Fantom core and
any 'fan.props' path dependencies. 'jig test' with no targets only runs
pods built into your workenv's own 'lib/fan/', and refuses to run outside
a workenv.

build [#buildCmd]
=================
Compile with the same output and status bar as tests:

  jig build                         # ./build.fan, else <workDir>/src/build.fan
  jig build acme                    # pod acme under the root build script
  jig build src/acme/build.fan      # a build script by path
  jig build -c                      # clean only
  jig build -f                      # clean, compile, then test
  jig build -t dist                 # run another build script target

A 'BuildGroup' builds all of its children, including nested groups.
'jig build' compiles by default; '-c', '-f', and '-t' each pick what runs
instead, so use only one.
Options:

  - '-c', '--clean': clean only, without compiling
  - '-f', '--full': clean and compile, then test every built pod that
    has tests; tests are skipped if any pod fails to compile
  - '-t', '--target <name>': run this build script target instead of
    compile
  - '-q', '--quiet': only print failures, status bar, and summary

Build output shows one line per pod with its compile time; the running
pod shows its current build step in dim text. Compiler warnings are
printed dim under their pod and counted in the summary, and errors are
printed above the summary. Indented output from custom build steps is
hidden. Rows come from top level '<target> [<pod>]' lines, so a custom
target that doesn't log one shows only in the status bar, and '-t test'
mixes 'fant' output in with the build; use 'jig test' for that.

Output [#output]
****************
**Default** prints one line per test class, grouped by pod:

  - '[passed/total]' counts, with columns aligned across all pods
  - 'pass' / 'fail' with elapsed time for the class ('4900ms' under 5sec,
    '7.2sec' under a minute, then '1min 2sec')
  - A 'FAIL: <method>' line under the class for each failed test
  - A summary line after each pod when the run covers more than one pod

Output from passing tests is hidden.

**Quiet** ('-q') prints a 'FAIL:' line with the full qualified name as each
test fails, and nothing for passing tests.

Both modes end with the details for every failure together, so you don't
have to hunt through scrollback: anything the test printed (prefixed with
'|') and the stack trace with test harness frames (reflection,
'TestRunner', 'verify' internals) removed. Then a one-line summary,
plus one line per failure showing how many tests in that class failed.

**Status bar.** When stdout is a terminal, a status bar on the bottom line
shows percent complete, tests run, pass/fail counts, total elapsed time,
and the current pod with its test progress, so you know where you are after
the pod header scrolls away. In default mode the class currently running
sits above it with live counts and a timer for the running method. Colors and the status bar are disabled when output is
piped, 'CI' is set, or 'TERM=dumb'.

Platform Support [#platforms]
*****************************
Jig is built for macOS and Linux terminals. It runs on Windows, but the
terminal output has rough edges:

  - **Colors and status bar.** Jig uses ANSI escape codes, which Windows
    Terminal supports but the legacy console does not. Use Windows Terminal,
    or set 'TERM=dumb' to get plain output.
  - **Symbols.** '✓ ✗ █ ░' need a UTF-8 console. Run 'chcp 65001' first if
    they show as garbled characters.
  - **Terminal width.** Width detection uses 'stty', which Windows lacks, so
    jig assumes 80 columns. Set 'COLUMNS' to your window width if long lines
    wrap.

Build Scripts [#build]
**********************
'BuildPod' runs tests with 'fant' in its 'test' target, which 'full' also
calls. Override 'test' to use jig instead:

  class Build : BuildPod
  {
    ...

    @Target { help = "Run the pod unit tests via jig" }
    override Void test()
    {
      fan := devHomeDir + (Env.cur.os == "win32" ? `bin/fan.bat` : `bin/fan`)
      Exec(this, [fan.osPath, "jig", "test", podName]).run
    }
  }

Failing tests fail the build. For a 'BuildGroup', override 'full' to clean
and compile every pod first, then test them all in a single jig run:

  class Build : BuildGroup
  {
    ...

    @Target { help = "Clean and compile all pods, then test all via jig" }
    Void full()
    {
      runOnChildren("clean")
      runOnChildren("compile")
      fan := devHomeDir + (Env.cur.os == "win32" ? `bin/fan.bat` : `bin/fan`)
      Exec(this, [fan.osPath, "jig", "test"].addAll(podNames(this))).run
    }

    ** Pod names of all BuildPod scripts in this group (recursive)
    private Str[] podNames(BuildScript s)
    {
      if (s is BuildPod) return [((BuildPod)s).podName]
      if (s is BuildGroup) return ((BuildGroup)s).children.map |c| { podNames(c) }.flatten
      return Str[,]
    }
  }

'Exec' captures jig's output, so in build scripts jig prints plain
output without colors or the status bar.

How it Works [#how]
*******************
Jig resolves targets up front (so typos fail fast and the status bar knows
the total), spawns a single 'fant' process, and parses its output as tests
run.
